package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Longer, suspense-led original serials with layered clues and complete endings. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch12 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries("the_house_of_second_chances", "The House of Second Chances", "A condemned neighbourhood building hides a remarkable invention—and a choice that could save a whole street.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "The Red Notice", listOf(
                "The red notice appeared on the door of Number 14 on a Monday morning. It gave the residents thirty days to leave before the old building was demolished. Most people folded their copies and went back to work. Fifteen-year-old Kamsi did not. Her mother ran the tiny repair shop downstairs, and the building was the only place Kamsi had ever called home.",
                "That afternoon, a stranger arrived carrying a photograph of the building taken nearly sixty years earlier. He asked whether anyone still remembered a carpenter named Mr Adeyemi. Kamsi recognised the name from the faded sign above the locked basement door. The stranger handed her a small wooden token and said, 'If the building is still standing, the promise may still be here.'"
            )),
            StoryPart(2, "The Basement Door", listOf(
                "The token fitted a shallow groove in the basement lock. Kamsi waited until her mother finished serving a customer, then asked the caretaker, Mr Bako, to come with her. The basement smelled of old paper and machine oil. Behind a stack of broken chairs stood a workbench covered by a sheet, and beneath it lay a metal box with three numbered dials.",
                "The stranger's photograph showed the same workbench, but the dials were arranged differently. Kamsi searched the room for a date or label and found a pencil mark beneath the bench: 4, 1, 9. The box opened to reveal a notebook, a bundle of blueprints, and a list of names—many belonging to families who still lived on the street."
            )),
            StoryPart(3, "A Machine for the Street", listOf(
                "The blueprints described a rainwater collection and filtration system designed for the entire block. It was clever, low-cost, and built from parts that local workshops could maintain. The notebook recorded how Mr Adeyemi had tested it with neighbours, then stopped after a page titled 'The night the pipes went silent.' No explanation followed, only a drawing of a cracked valve.",
                "Kamsi showed the plans to her science teacher, who warned her not to assume the design was safe simply because it was old. They would need an engineer to inspect it and the residents' permission before attempting anything. The teacher recognised the system's unusual pressure-control idea and suggested contacting a retired civil engineer named Mrs Nwosu, whose name appeared on the blueprint margin."
            )),
            StoryPart(4, "The Engineer's Question", listOf(
                "Mrs Nwosu arrived with a measuring tape, a notebook, and no patience for guesswork. She examined the drawings and explained that the system had been ahead of its time, but some materials would need replacing. The cracked valve was not the only concern: the original design relied on a maintenance rota that had apparently fallen apart after a disagreement.",
                "She asked Kamsi why she cared so much about a building scheduled for demolition. Kamsi admitted that she was afraid the families would be scattered and the street would lose the small ways neighbours helped one another. Mrs Nwosu studied the old list of names. 'Then find out why they stopped maintaining it,' she said. 'A machine can be repaired. Trust takes more careful work.'"
            )),
            StoryPart(5, "The Missing Page", listOf(
                "The notebook jumped from the first successful test to the night the pipes went silent. Kamsi and her friend Femi searched the archive for reports about the building. They found a brief newspaper item about a flood, but the date did not match the notebook. A shopkeeper remembered that Mr Adeyemi had argued with the residents' committee over who should control the system.",
                "In the back of the metal box, Femi found a thin strip of paper stuck beneath the lining. It was a torn piece of the missing page. The visible words read: 'I will not sign away the keys while the repair fund is...' The rest had been ripped off. Kamsi realised the mystery might not be about a failed machine at all. It might be about money, ownership, and a promise that someone had misunderstood."
            )),
            StoryPart(6, "The People on the List", listOf(
                "Kamsi visited the older residents one by one, taking the notebook but never accusing anyone. Mrs Danjuma remembered the repair fund and said every family had contributed what they could. Mr Bello recalled that the committee treasurer had moved away suddenly. A retired teacher, Mr Sanni, remembered a meeting where Mr Adeyemi refused to hand over the keys because the accounts did not balance.",
                "The details did not agree, but one fact kept returning: the fund had been placed in a bank account until an independent inspection could be completed. Nobody knew what happened afterward. Kamsi asked the residents to search their own papers. By evening, three people had found receipts, and one had a letter addressed to the building's former caretaker."
            )),
            StoryPart(7, "The Account Book", listOf(
                "The letter led them to a storage office at the old market, where the caretaker's daughter kept family documents. Inside a tin folder was an account book showing every contribution and every purchase. The money had not been stolen. It had been used to buy replacement parts, but the delivery was cancelled when the supplier closed. A refund cheque was recorded on the final page.",
                "The cheque had been returned because the committee's address was outdated. Mrs Nwosu checked the bank's historical records through the proper process and confirmed that the funds had later been transferred to an inactive community account. The amount was modest, but enough to begin a safe pilot. Kamsi felt relief, then noticed a signature on the transfer form that she had seen before: Mr Bako's."
            )),
            StoryPart(8, "The Caretaker's Truth", listOf(
                "Mr Bako did not deny signing the form. He explained that he had been a young assistant, asked to move the money into a protected account while the committee sorted out the dispute. He had kept the documents but never knew the account could be reclaimed. He had also been too embarrassed to tell anyone that he had lost the follow-up letter.",
                "Kamsi was upset that he had stayed silent, but Mrs Nwosu reminded her that blame would not recover the building. Mr Bako offered to help trace the account and present the records to the residents. At the next meeting, he apologised publicly. The residents agreed to work together, provided the project had transparent accounts and independent safety checks."
            )),
            StoryPart(9, "Thirty Days", listOf(
                "The engineer inspected the basement, tested the old pipe route, and prepared a revised plan. The system could not supply drinking water without a much larger certified treatment process, but it could safely collect rainwater for cleaning shared spaces and watering the street garden. That smaller purpose still mattered. The residents formed a registered cooperative, with published costs and a rotating maintenance team.",
                "Kamsi presented the plan at the council hearing. She did not promise miracles; she showed the inspection report, the funding records, and the residents' signatures. The council agreed to delay demolition while the building's structural repairs were independently assessed. On the twenty-ninth day, the red notice was replaced with a notice announcing a review. It was not a victory yet, but it was time."
            )),
            StoryPart(10, "A Door Left Open", listOf(
                "The structural report found that the building could be made safe with repairs. The cooperative secured a grant, and the rainwater system passed its limited-use inspection. The basement became a shared workshop where residents learned to maintain the equipment and repair everyday items. Mr Bako kept the original notebook in a glass case, beside a clear account of what had gone wrong and how the community had corrected it.",
                "The stranger returned for the reopening. He introduced himself as Mr Adeyemi's grandson and said the wooden token had been meant for whoever cared enough to ask questions. Kamsi gave him a copy of the restored plans. Number 14 was no longer simply an old building waiting to be cleared; it was a place with a future, built by people who had learned that second chances work best when everyone can see the truth."
            ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_signal_in_room_27", "The Signal in Room 27", "A school radio club follows a strange broadcast and discovers who has been quietly keeping the town connected.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "Static at Lunch", listOf(
                "The school radio club had been given Room 27 because nobody else wanted it. The windows rattled, the microphone cable was missing, and the only working radio produced a soft hiss. Then, during lunch on Thursday, a voice cut through the static and read three numbers: 12, 4, 8. After that came a melody played on what sounded like a toy piano.",
                "Ayo recorded the broadcast on his phone, while Sade checked the school timetable for anything scheduled at those times. There was nothing. Their teacher told them not to trespass or chase rumours, but agreed they could investigate the signal through public records and a supervised visit to the town's community station. The next broadcast ended with one sentence: 'Tell the person who remembers the bridge.'"
            )),
            StoryPart(2, "Three Numbers", listOf(
                "The numbers matched shelves in the school library, but shelf twelve held atlases, shelf four held old newspapers, and shelf eight held science magazines. Sade found a folded clipping tucked inside a 1998 newspaper. It described a storm that had damaged the town's footbridge and interrupted emergency announcements for several hours.",
                "A photograph showed volunteers carrying a portable radio between the clinic and the market. One volunteer had a school badge pinned to his shirt. The caption named him as Samuel Dike, a former student who later helped establish the community radio station. Ayo wondered whether the broadcast was a recording from the past, but the date on the radio display changed with every transmission."
            )),
            StoryPart(3, "The Bridge Remembered", listOf(
                "Their teacher contacted the community station and arranged a visit. The station manager, Mrs Ibe, listened to the recording twice. She recognised the melody as the opening tune of an old volunteer programme, but said the station no longer used that frequency. She also explained that the signal was weak and could be coming from a low-power device nearby.",
                "Mrs Ibe found Samuel Dike's name in an old volunteer register. He had retired after a disagreement about how the station should preserve its archive. Before leaving, he had asked the station to keep a particular recording private until the people in it could be contacted. The manager had assumed the recording was lost. Ayo noticed a fresh pencil mark beside the register entry: Room 27."
            )),
            StoryPart(4, "The Locked Cabinet", listOf(
                "Room 27 had once been the school's broadcast room, long before the radio club existed. Behind a cupboard, the caretaker found a narrow cabinet with a broken label. The key was kept in the school's records office, and the head teacher gave permission for the club to inspect it with an adult present.",
                "Inside were reels, a hand-drawn map of the town, and a logbook. The final entry said that a backup transmitter had been installed after the bridge storm so that clinic staff could receive messages if the main network failed. The transmitter was supposed to have been removed years later. Ayo looked at the map and saw a small X beside the old water tower."
            )),
            StoryPart(5, "The Voice on the Tape", listOf(
                "The club borrowed an approved player from the station and listened to the oldest reel. The recording began with the same piano melody, then a young voice calmly guided volunteers to the clinic. It was not a secret code; it was a record of people helping one another when normal communication had failed. Near the end, someone said, 'If this is ever played again, make sure the names are remembered.'",
                "The names were partly obscured by damage. Sade used the volunteer register to identify most of them, but one name had been crossed out. Mrs Ibe explained that the person had asked not to be publicly recognised after a difficult family situation. The club agreed that remembering people did not mean exposing anyone who had chosen privacy."
            )),
            StoryPart(6, "A Visit to the Water Tower", listOf(
                "With permission from the council and the station manager, the club visited the old water tower. A maintenance worker showed them the fenced equipment shed and explained that the transmitter had been disconnected, though a small monitoring device remained. Its battery was nearly empty. It had been sending a scheduled test tone whenever it detected a signal fault.",
                "The numbers in the broadcast were not coordinates. They were the last three entries in the old maintenance log, and the piano tune was a stored test recording. Someone had recently powered the monitor while repairing the tower's electrical system. The mystery now had a practical explanation, but one question remained: who had programmed the message about the bridge?"
            )),
            StoryPart(7, "Samuel's Notebook", listOf(
                "Mrs Ibe found a second notebook in the station archive. Samuel had written that the bridge message should only be replayed when the town was preparing to replace the old footbridge. The recording included accounts from residents who had helped during the storm and a reminder that the new bridge should include a safe communication point for emergencies.",
                "The council had recently announced a bridge renovation, which explained why Samuel's grandson, who worked on the tower, had restored the monitoring device. He had not intended to frighten anyone; he wanted the archive to be heard before the equipment was removed. The club contacted him through the station and asked whether he would help them prepare a public history programme."
            )),
            StoryPart(8, "The Missing Name", listOf(
                "Samuel's grandson, Chidi, arrived carrying a sealed envelope from his grandfather. Inside was a note explaining why one volunteer's name had been crossed out: she had been a young person escaping a dangerous home situation and had later built a new life elsewhere. Samuel had promised not to publish her identity without permission.",
                "The club chose to honour the volunteer group collectively and preserve the private name in a restricted archive. Chidi thanked them for understanding. He said his grandfather had worried that people would turn the recording into a mystery instead of hearing its real message: ordinary neighbours had kept one another informed when it mattered."
            )),
            StoryPart(9, "Broadcast Night", listOf(
                "The school and community station organised a broadcast about the bridge, the storm, and the volunteers. The programme included the recovered recording, interviews with residents who consented, and a clear explanation of the technical signal. Ayo and Sade presented the story without exaggerating it, and Mrs Ibe checked every date before it went on air.",
                "During the programme, a caller corrected one detail about the clinic's location. Rather than hide the mistake, Sade thanked the caller and updated the online transcript. The station received messages from former volunteers and younger listeners who wanted to join. The broadcast had done more than solve a puzzle; it had brought a scattered history back into conversation."
            )),
            StoryPart(10, "A Clear Frequency", listOf(
                "The council included an emergency communication point in the bridge renovation plans. The station digitised the recording with permission and stored a public version that protected the volunteer's identity. Room 27 became a proper student studio, complete with a schedule, safety rules, and a mentor from the community station.",
                "On the first day of the new club, the radio produced no mysterious static. It carried Sade's voice introducing a programme about local history. Ayo placed the old logbook beside the microphone, open to the line about remembering the bridge. The signal had never been a ghost calling from the past. It was a practical reminder that communities stay connected when people listen carefully—and when they make room for every voice that can safely be heard."
            ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_museum_of_unfinished_things", "The Museum of Unfinished Things", "Three young volunteers uncover the stories behind abandoned inventions and help their creators finish what they started.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "The Empty Museum", listOf(
                "The town museum was almost always empty on Wednesdays. Its newest exhibit, the Museum of Unfinished Things, contained a bicycle with square wheels, a half-painted mural, a clock with no hands, and a wooden bird that could not fly. Visitors laughed at the odd collection, but volunteer guide Lami thought the objects looked more hopeful than foolish.",
                "While dusting the display, she found a card that had slipped behind the clock. It read: 'Do not finish these for us. Find out why we stopped.' The card was signed with three initials: M, R, and T. The museum director said the collection had been donated anonymously, and no one knew who had made the pieces."
            )),
            StoryPart(2, "The Bicycle That Wouldn't Roll", listOf(
                "Lami invited her friends Peter and Nneka to help research the exhibits. The bicycle's maker had left a workshop stamp on the frame, leading them to a repair shop run by an elderly mechanic named Mr Tunde. He remembered designing a demonstration bicycle for a school project, but the team had abandoned it after a sponsor demanded that they remove the safety features to make it faster.",
                "Mr Tunde showed them the original sketches. The square wheels were not a failed attempt at an ordinary bicycle; they were part of a moving display designed to teach children about curves and rolling motion. The team had stopped when the sponsor withdrew support. Lami realised the objects were not random failures. Each one had a story about a decision."
            )),
            StoryPart(3, "The Mural Under Paper", listOf(
                "The half-painted mural was covered by layers of protective paper. The museum's conservator helped the volunteers lift one corner and discovered a scene of the old market, with tiny portraits of stallholders hidden in the crowd. A signature near the bottom belonged to R. Musa, a sign painter who had moved away after the market was rebuilt.",
                "Nneka found an interview in a local newspaper. Musa had wanted to include the names of every stallholder, but the redevelopment committee asked him to remove the portraits because the wall was meant to advertise the new shops. He stopped painting rather than erase the people who had made the market what it was. The museum director agreed to contact him before any restoration began."
            )),
            StoryPart(4, "The Clock Without Hands", listOf(
                "The clock had a beautiful wooden case but no hands and no numbers. Peter discovered a tiny inscription inside the back panel: 'Time belongs to the people who use it.' The museum's records linked the clock to a community centre that had once offered free lessons, meals, and after-school activities.",
                "The centre's founder, Mrs Taiwo, explained that the clock had been designed by her brother. He wanted it to chime at the times when volunteers changed shifts, not to tell people when they were late. When the centre closed for repairs, the clock was packed away and its hands were removed so nobody would mistake it for a working timepiece. The unfinished object was a reminder of a place that had not yet reopened."
            )),
            StoryPart(5, "The Wooden Bird", listOf(
                "The wooden bird was carved with extraordinary care, but its wings were fixed. A label mentioned a craft teacher named Mr Ekanem, who had taught woodworking to students for decades. He now lived outside town and rarely visited the museum. Lami wrote a letter explaining that the bird had been found among the unfinished collection.",
                "Mr Ekanem replied with a photograph of a classroom full of wooden birds. His students had designed them as part of a lesson about flight, balance, and the difference between a model and a real animal. One student had wanted to make the bird flap, but the class decided not to build a mechanism they could not test safely. The bird was complete as a teaching model, even if it never flew."
            )),
            StoryPart(6, "Three Initials", listOf(
                "The initials on the card belonged to the museum's former director, a retired teacher, and a community organiser. They had gathered the objects to challenge the idea that unfinished work was always wasted. The collection was meant to ask visitors what happens when a project changes direction, when a person chooses safety over applause, or when a community refuses to erase its history.",
                "Lami felt embarrassed that she had assumed every object needed fixing. The director explained that the museum had lost the note describing the exhibit's purpose during a staff change. The volunteers proposed a new display in which each object would be accompanied by the maker's account, including the reasons it had remained as it was."
            )),
            StoryPart(7, "The Makers Return", listOf(
                "Mr Tunde agreed to bring the bicycle's original demonstration pieces. R. Musa returned to see the mural and asked to repaint it with the market stallholders' names, after obtaining their permission. Mrs Taiwo offered photographs of the community centre, and Mr Ekanem sent lesson plans showing how the wooden bird had been used in class.",
                "Not everyone wanted to return. One maker had passed away, and another preferred not to be interviewed. The volunteers respected those boundaries and used only records the museum had permission to display. Their project became more thoughtful: a museum should preserve stories, not demand them from people."
            )),
            StoryPart(8, "A Different Kind of Opening", listOf(
                "The museum prepared a new exhibition called 'Work That Changed Along the Way.' Visitors could turn a wheel to see how the bicycle demonstration worked, listen to a recording of the mural painter describing the market, and read about the community centre's volunteer rota. The wooden bird remained exactly as it was, with a note explaining why the class had chosen a non-moving model.",
                "On preview day, a child asked whether the unfinished clock could be repaired. Mrs Taiwo smiled and said it could, but she wanted the community centre to reopen first so the clock could return to the place it had been made for. The child asked whether that meant the clock was waiting. 'Yes,' said Lami. 'And waiting can be part of a good plan.'"
            )),
            StoryPart(9, "The Centre's Keys", listOf(
                "The museum's publicity brought attention to the closed community centre. Former volunteers met with the council and discovered that the building needed repairs, not demolition. A local foundation offered materials, while residents organised supervised workdays. The clock's maker had left a maintenance guide, which Mrs Taiwo had kept with her papers.",
                "Lami and her friends helped catalogue donated books and games, but they did not take on work beyond their training. Qualified tradespeople handled electrical and structural repairs. When the centre passed inspection, the volunteers placed the clock on its old wall, still without hands, beside a new sign explaining its purpose."
            )),
            StoryPart(10, "Nothing Wasted", listOf(
                "The community centre reopened with a reading room, a small workshop, and a quiet space for anyone who needed help with schoolwork. The museum kept the bicycle, mural sketches, and wooden bird, while the clock returned to the centre where it could mark the rhythm of shared service. Each object had found a place without being forced into a different shape.",
                "At the exhibition's closing event, Lami read the card aloud and added one final sentence beneath it: 'An unfinished thing is not always a failure; sometimes it is a question waiting for the right people.' The visitors left discussing the projects they had abandoned and the ones they might restart. Lami understood that progress was not simply finishing everything. It was learning what deserved to be finished, what deserved to change, and what deserved to be remembered."
            ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_mapmaker_of_morning", "The Mapmaker of Morning", "A young cartographer maps the overlooked paths of her city and finds a way to protect the people who depend on them.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "A Map with Blank Spaces", listOf(
                "When Eniola joined the city mapping competition, she expected to draw roads, bus stops, and public buildings. Her grandfather, a retired survey assistant, gave her an old map and asked her to look for what it left out. The map showed a wide avenue where Eniola knew there was a crowded pedestrian lane, and it marked a vacant lot where a busy morning market stood.",
                "Her grandfather explained that maps could be accurate and still tell an incomplete story. Eniola began a notebook of overlooked routes: the shortcut used by nurses, the ramp built by shopkeepers, the footpath children took to reach the library. The competition deadline was three weeks away, and she had no idea how to fit a living city onto one sheet."
            )),
            StoryPart(2, "The Market Before Sunrise", listOf(
                "Eniola woke before dawn to observe the market. Traders arrived with handcarts, cleaners cleared the drains, and a baker carried trays through a lane missing from the official map. She asked permission before taking notes and learned that the lane flooded whenever rubbish blocked a narrow culvert.",
                "A trader named Mama Bisi showed her a hand-drawn route used by delivery riders. It included a safe crossing behind the clinic and a public tap that did not appear on any council map. Eniola copied the route carefully, then noticed a red mark beside the culvert. Mama Bisi said it had been there for months, but nobody had explained what it meant."
            )),
            StoryPart(3, "The Red Mark", listOf(
                "The red mark was a survey symbol indicating a proposed drainage project. At the council office, Eniola learned that the plan would widen the avenue and close the pedestrian lane for construction. The project had a legitimate safety purpose, but the published drawing did not show the market's delivery route or the clinic crossing.",
                "Her grandfather warned her not to assume the planners had ignored people deliberately. Maps were often made from older surveys, and local knowledge had to be checked. Eniola decided to gather evidence: photographs with permission, measured distances, and interviews from people who used the lane at different times of day."
            )),
            StoryPart(4, "The Route Nobody Recorded", listOf(
                "The clinic's head nurse confirmed that the lane was used by patients who could not manage the steep avenue steps. A school crossing guard showed Eniola where children waited when traffic became heavy. A delivery rider explained that closing the lane without an alternative would force carts onto a narrow road.",
                "Eniola added each route to a transparent draft map, marking which details had been measured and which came from interviews. She included dates and asked contributors to check their statements. The map was becoming crowded, but it showed something the official plan did not: one construction decision could affect several groups in different ways."
            )),
            StoryPart(5, "A Map Is Not a Vote", listOf(
                "At the first community meeting, a few residents accused Eniola of trying to stop the drainage project. She explained that she supported safer drainage; she wanted the design to account for the paths people relied on. The engineer in charge listened but said the project could not be changed based on stories alone.",
                "Her grandfather suggested a field walk with the engineer, the market association, the clinic, and the school. They agreed to meet at sunrise, when the lane was busiest. Eniola prepared a printed map with numbered observation points, making sure everyone could add corrections rather than simply accept her version."
            )),
            StoryPart(6, "The Walk at Dawn", listOf(
                "During the field walk, the engineer saw the delivery carts, the clinic crossing, and the flooded culvert. He admitted that the survey team had visited in the afternoon, when the lane was almost empty. The missing details were not in the data because nobody had collected them at the right time.",
                "The engineer asked Eniola to show where a temporary accessible route could fit during construction. The group identified a service road that could be improved with a ramp and clear signs. It would cost more and require coordination, but it could keep the clinic and market connected while the drainage work continued."
            )),
            StoryPart(7, "The Map Changes", listOf(
                "Eniola updated the map with the temporary route, the culvert, the market's delivery times, and the school crossing. She used different line styles for confirmed measurements, community reports, and proposed changes. Her grandfather checked the scale, while the engineer reviewed the technical details.",
                "One interviewee asked to have their name removed because they feared conflict with a landlord. Eniola replaced the name with a general description and kept the original consent form secure with the teacher supervising the project. She learned that a good map needed not only accuracy but also care for the people represented on it."
            )),
            StoryPart(8, "The Competition", listOf(
                "At the competition, the judges asked why Eniola had included paths that were not official roads. She explained that maps were tools for decisions, and decisions improved when planners could see how people actually moved. She also described the limits of her work: it was a community observation map, not a legal survey or engineering approval.",
                "The judges praised her method and asked the city engineer to comment. He confirmed that the field walk had identified a genuine gap in the project survey. The council agreed to review the temporary access plan before construction began. Eniola won a special award for public-interest mapping, but the most important result was still ahead."
            )),
            StoryPart(9, "A Better Construction Plan", listOf(
                "The revised plan kept the drainage project and added a safe temporary route, accessible ramps, signs, and scheduled delivery windows. The market association agreed to keep the culvert clear, while the council committed to regular inspections. The clinic received advance notice of each construction stage so it could guide visitors.",
                "Eniola's map was displayed at the market office with a date and a note explaining how to report changes. Some paths would shift as work progressed, so the map included a way to submit corrections. Instead of pretending one drawing could remain perfect forever, it showed how the community could keep it useful."
            )),
            StoryPart(10, "The City Wakes", listOf(
                "The drainage work finished before the rainy season. The lane reopened with a safer crossing and a repaired culvert. The market still began before sunrise, the clinic still received visitors, and the schoolchildren had a clear route that did not force them into traffic. The city added Eniola's community mapping method to its next neighbourhood survey.",
                "Her grandfather framed the first map beside the revised one. The blank spaces had not been mistakes to hide; they had been questions to ask. Eniola entered the next competition with a new idea: a map that residents could update together. Each morning, as the city woke, she learned another route, another name, and another reason that a place mattered."
            ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_archive_of_tomorrows", "The Archive of Tomorrows", "A group of students builds a time capsule for the future and discovers the most important thing to preserve is still being created.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "A Letter from 2075", listOf(
                "The letter arrived at the school in a plain envelope with no stamp. It was addressed to 'The students who will still be here when the roof is repaired.' Inside, someone claiming to write from the year 2075 asked the school to preserve one ordinary object from 2026. The head teacher suspected a publicity stunt, but the date and a tiny school emblem matched an old design kept in the archive.",
                "Four students—Mina, Yusuf, Chika, and Ben—were asked to investigate as part of the school's anniversary project. They found a note on the back: 'Do not choose the most expensive thing. Choose what will help us understand how you lived.' The team had one month to create a time capsule, and suddenly every everyday object seemed important."
            )),
            StoryPart(2, "The Ordinary Things List", listOf(
                "The students made a list of objects: a bus ticket, a lunch wrapper, a seed packet, a class timetable, a handwritten recipe, and a broken phone charger. Their teacher asked them to think about context. A ticket without a route meant little; a recipe without the cook's permission might reveal a family detail that should remain private.",
                "Mina proposed recording short interviews with volunteers about why each object mattered. Yusuf suggested including the school's current rules and timetable, while Chika wanted to preserve the sound of the morning assembly. Ben worried that digital files might become unreadable. They needed a plan that would make sense to someone who had never seen their world."
            )),
            StoryPart(3, "The First Interview", listOf(
                "Their first interview was with the school cleaner, Mrs Okoro, who had worked there for twenty-six years. She chose a small broom handle, worn smooth where her hand rested. It was not valuable, she explained, but it reminded her of the day students helped clean after a storm instead of leaving the work to staff.",
                "The team recorded her story with permission and wrote down the date, location, and names of those who wished to be credited. They began to see that the archive was not a collection of objects; it was a collection of choices people had made together. The next interview, however, brought a disagreement they had not expected."
            )),
            StoryPart(4, "The Argument About the Trophy", listOf(
                "The sports captain wanted the time capsule to include the school's biggest trophy. He argued that future students should know the team had won a regional championship. Chika pointed out that the trophy already appeared in photographs and that it told only one part of the school's story.",
                "Yusuf suggested including the team captain's training diary, but the captain did not want private notes displayed. They agreed to ask the whole team what they wanted remembered. The players chose a team photograph, a public match programme, and a short account of how they organised transport for students who could not afford to travel."
            )),
            StoryPart(5, "The Object That Wasn't There", listOf(
                "While cataloguing the archive room, Ben found a blank space on an old shelf and a label reading 'Future Garden.' The school had once planned a garden but never built it. An old letter showed that students had collected seeds, then lost the site when the land was reassigned for storage.",
                "The team visited the proposed site and found it was now a patch of hard ground behind the library. Their science teacher said a small container garden might still be possible, but they would need permission, a water plan, and someone to maintain it during holidays. Mina suggested that perhaps the time capsule should include something that was not finished yet."
            )),
            StoryPart(6, "A Promise with Conditions", listOf(
                "The head teacher approved a pilot garden with clear conditions: no digging near the building foundations, no unsafe tools for students, and an adult supervisor for maintenance. The students created a rota and asked the nearby nursery for advice on suitable plants. They recorded the plan, including the possibility that some plants might not survive.",
                "Ben worried that including a living plant in a time capsule was impossible. Chika answered that the capsule did not have to contain the garden itself; it could contain the first seed packet, a photograph, and the names of the people who promised to care for it. The future would receive not only an object but evidence of a project in progress."
            )),
            StoryPart(7, "The Envelope's Secret", listOf(
                "The archivist examined the mysterious letter and found that its paper came from a stationery set used by the school decades earlier. The writing was modern, but the emblem had been copied from a 1975 anniversary badge. The letter was not from the future. It was a challenge created by a former student who wanted the school to think beyond medals and buildings.",
                "The archivist found the student's name in an old alumni newsletter: Dr Nkiru Eze, now a historian. She confirmed that she had written the letter as part of a project about how communities imagine the future. She had hoped the students would question the message rather than believe it blindly. The team decided to include the truth about the letter in the archive."
            )),
            StoryPart(8, "What Should Last", listOf(
                "The students invited staff, families, and neighbours to a small exhibition. Visitors voted for the objects they felt best represented daily life, but the team made sure the final selection included different ages and experiences. The broom handle, bus ticket, recipe, team photograph, seed packet, and a printed digital safety guide all made the list.",
                "They also included a short audio collection in two formats, a printed transcript, and instructions for future archivists to check the files every few years. Personal stories were included only with clear permission. The archive was becoming less like a box of treasures and more like a set of careful instructions for people they would never meet."
            )),
            StoryPart(9, "The Day of the Capsule", listOf(
                "At the anniversary ceremony, each contributor placed an approved item into the capsule and explained why it mattered. Mrs Okoro placed a new broom handle beside the worn one, showing that the work of caring for a school continued. The sports team added its programme, and the garden group included the seed packet and its maintenance plan.",
                "The head teacher sealed the capsule in a protected display cabinet with a date for its next review. The students also uploaded a public catalogue that avoided private information and explained how future classes could add their own records. Ben placed a copy of the original letter beside the catalogue, now labelled 'A question from the past, not a message from the future.'"
            )),
            StoryPart(10, "The Future Is a Verb", listOf(
                "A year later, the garden was thriving, though two plants had been replaced and the rota had changed. The archive catalogue recorded those changes instead of pretending the original plan had worked perfectly. Younger students interviewed the first garden team and added their own account of what they had learned.",
                "When Mina read the letter again, she understood its cleverness. The writer had not asked them to predict the future or preserve something famous. She had asked them to leave evidence that people had cared, adapted, and kept learning. The capsule would be opened decades later, but the archive of tomorrows was already growing—in every student who chose to add a truthful page."
            ))
        ))
    )
}
